3461065,"_gp_pool_share":true,"_score_pool_share":100.0,"_gate_rev_durability":null,"_gp_rev_durability":null,"_score_rev_durability":0.0,"_gate_fcf_durability":null,"_gp_fcf_durability":null,"_score_fcf_durability":null,"_gate_margins":null,"_gp_margins":null,"_score_margins":null,"_gate_fund_growth":null,"_gp_fund_growth":null,"_score_fund_growth":null,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.04848735884812437,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":true,"_score_shrhldr_yield":65.0,"_gate_share_shrink":null,"_gp_share_shrink":null,"_score_share_shrink":0.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.17424069828093458,"_gp_sbc_dilution":false,"_score_sbc_dilution":0.0,"_gate_insider_own":0.05139,"_gp_insider_own":true,"_score_insider_own":34.3,"_gate_insider_buying":null,"_gp_insider_buying":null,"_score_insider_buying":null,"pf":[],"description_full":"TPG Inc. operates as an alternative asset manager in the United States and internationally. The company provides investment management services to TPG Funds, limited partners, separately managed accounts and clients, and other vehicles; advisory, debt and equity arrangement, and underwriting and placement services; and capital structuring and other advice services. In addition, it invests in private equity funds, real estate funds, hedge funds, and credit funds. It also offers rental services for various household items, cars, office machines, and equipment. TPG Inc. was founded in 1992 and is based in Fort Worth, Texas.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Jon  Winkelried | CEO & Director | Age 65 | Compensation: $16.3M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 1K people. Revenue per employee of $2.5M ranks in the top quartile among Financial Services peers, with revenue growing 33%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $528K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $428K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 7\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (10/10).","financial_summary":["TPG Inc. is a large-cap financial services business with a $18.6B market cap on $4.7B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +33% over the past three years, the kind of sustained demand persistence that marks a compounder.","Returns on capital run at 41% against a 11% cost of capital \u2014 a 30-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat.","The business throws off $1.0B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 21 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 5.59\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 1.0\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and interest coverage runs at 7.5\u00d7.","Shareholder yield of 4.8% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and stock-based comp at 17% of revenue is silent dilution an owner has to account for, and insiders hold 5.1% of the shares."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 112% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":1.121771212319649},{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (1.7) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Payout ratio looks stretched \u2014 the dividend\u2019s own margin of safety is thin","sev":"red","cat":"dividend"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Operating margins 13% below sector median \u2014 a cost disadvantage the moat cannot hide","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 9.4% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Credit cycles and regulatory capital \u2014 losses accumulate invisibly in good years and appear all at once, and the time to worry is when underwriting standards are loosest.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Fintech disintermediation \u2014 neobanks, embedded finance, and blockchain rails are unbundling the fee streams that traditional banks took for granted.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Interest rate regime shifts \u2014 a rapid move in rates can invert the yield curve, crush bond portfolios, and trigger deposit flight simultaneously.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Founder-led \u2014 owner alignment that often matters more than any incentive plan","ROIC sits in the top quartile of Financial Services peers \u2014 the moat is wider than the neighbourhood","Growing faster than most Financial Services peers \u2014 taking share inside the profit pool","Minimal goodwill on the balance sheet \u2014 low impairment risk"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"TPG Telecom (ASX:TPG) Shares in Focus: Why Is the ASX 200 Stock on the Radar?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/au/stocks/communication/tpg-telecom-asxtpg-shares-in-focus-why-is-the-asx-200-stock-on-the-radar-1","date":"2026-11-13","timestamp":1794552840.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.077,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"TPG Telecom (ASX:TPG) Shares in Focus: Why Is the ASX 200 Stock Drawing Attention?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/au/stocks/communication/tpg-telecom-asxtpg-shares-in-focus-why-is-the-asx-200-stock-drawing-attention","date":"2026-11-13","timestamp":1794552780.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.743,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"TPG Telecom (ASX:TPG) Network Sharing and Retention in the Spotlight","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/au/stocks/communication/tpg-telecom-asxtpg-network-sharing-and-retention-in-the-spotlight","date":"2026-11-1